    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start, but can you go into a bit more detail on the specific steps within that enzymatic breakdown? I'd like to explain to the students why it happens so suddenly once the knife hits the onion—is there something about the way the cells rupture or the way the gas disperses that makes the sensation feel so immediate and sharp?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The suddenness comes from the compartmentalization of the reactants within the onion cells; the alliinase and alliin are normally separated. When the cell walls are ruptured by the knife, these components mix rapidly, triggering the enzyme's action almost instantaneously. The resulting syn-propanethial S-oxide is a highly volatile gas that immediately diffuses into the air, and its small molecular size allows it to quickly reach and irritate the sensitive mucous membranes of the eye. This rapid dissolution and dispersal are key to the sharp, immediate sensation.",
      "source": "target",
